---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import DocumentationNav from '../components/DocumentationNav.astro';
import PageBreadcrumb from '../components/shell/PageBreadcrumb.astro';
import { canonicalUrl, urlPolicy } from '../data/url-policy';
const sections = [
  { id: 'addresses', label: 'Address contract' },
  { id: 'discovery', label: 'Search and AI discovery' },
  { id: 'migration', label: 'Migration and extension' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="URL policy"
  description="GenUX URL contract: stable readable addresses, canonical ownership, search discovery and compatible route migrations."
  slug="url-policy"
>
  <PageBreadcrumb
    items={[
      { label: 'Overview', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Project', href: '/project/' },
    ]}
    current="URL policy"
  />
  <h1>URL policy</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    People should be able to read, share and revisit an address. Each published document has one
    stable canonical URL, shared by navigation, metadata and discovery feeds.
  </p>
  <DocumentationNav items={sections} />
  <section id="addresses" data-doc-section="addresses" aria-labelledby="addresses-title">
    <h2 id="addresses-title">Address contract</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Use lowercase, meaningful English words separated by hyphens. Documentation routes end in a
        slash; files retain their extensions.
      </li>
      <li>
        Keep existing descriptive addresses stable. Categories organize navigation without forcing a
        migration of every child URL.
      </li>
      <li>
        Components live at <code>/components/&lt;component-name&gt;/</code>. Use fragments for
        sections within a document.
      </li>
      <li>
        View controls, filters and <code>?tab=</code> state do not create separate canonical
        documents. The canonical address excludes query strings and fragments.
      </li>
      <li>
        The public base is <a href={canonicalUrl('/')}>{canonicalUrl('/')}</a>. Local preview and
        public hosting use the same logical routes.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="discovery" data-doc-section="discovery" aria-labelledby="discovery-title">
    <h2 id="discovery-title">Search and AI discovery</h2>
    <p>
      References and directory links are rendered in HTML. Each document has a descriptive title,
      summary and absolute canonical address. Structured data describes the visible page and
      breadcrumbs.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a href="/sitemap.xml">Sitemap</a> lists canonical documentation only.{' '}
      <a href="/url-manifest.json">URL manifest</a> exposes route ownership and aliases.{' '}
      <a href="/llms.txt">Text discovery index</a> is a convenience for agents, not a search-engine
      requirement or a ranking guarantee.
    </p>
    <p>
      Isolated previews and retired aliases remain outside the sitemap and carry noindex.
      Code/source resources are supporting files, not additional documentation pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      English is the server-rendered canonical language. The Turkish display toggle uses the same
      URL. Separate language routes and reciprocal hreflang will be added only when actual
      translated HTML is published.
    </p>
    <p>
      GitHub project Pages cannot control the host-root robots.txt. A file below /genux/ does not
      set crawler policy for atonota.github.io. Search discovery and model training permissions are
      separate decisions.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="migration" data-doc-section="migration" aria-labelledby="migration-title">
    <h2 id="migration-title">Migration and extension</h2>
    <p>
      Register a new document in the shared URL policy, admit its page type, link it from a relevant
      directory and verify the generated public-base artifact. Retire a route only with an explicit
      direct alias to a real canonical target.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {Object.entries(urlPolicy.aliases).map(([old, target]) => (
        <li>
          <code>{old}</code> → <a href={target}>{target}</a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Aliases on static GitHub Pages use client navigation and a no-script refresh with a fallback
      link. They are not HTTP 301 responses. Existing token-section fragment mappings are preserved.
      An old repository’s hosting path requires a redirect deployed in that repository.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unknown paths show a recovery page with HTTP 404 rather than silently replacing every missing
      document with the homepage.
    </p>
    <p>
      References:{' '}
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/url-structure">
        Google URL structure
      </a>
      ,{' '}
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing/consolidate-duplicate-urls">
        canonicalization
      </a>
      ,{' '}
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/ai-features">
        AI search guidance
      </a>
      , <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/bots">OpenAI crawler controls</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
